  "context": [
    "The E-Class sold in India is exclusively the long-wheelbase body, a market-specific decision Mercedes-Benz has carried across generations because Indian buyers overwhelmingly use the car as a chauffeur-driven rear-seat executive sedan rather than a driver's car. The current W214 generation arrived in India in 2024, replacing the W213 that had been on sale since 2017, and is offered as the E 200 petrol and E 220d diesel, both paired exclusively with a 9-speed 9G-TRONIC automatic.",
    "As a fully imported, CKD-assembled luxury product, the E-Class carries India's steep luxury-vehicle duty structure baked into its price, and that same structure works against it at resale: used buyers at this price point can often step into a newer entry-luxury model or a well-kept previous-generation E-Class for similar money, which caps how much a used W214 can command once the initial ownership cycle ends.",
    "Resale value falls off faster once the standard new-car warranty period lapses, because secondhand buyers price in the real cost of out-of-warranty service, parts, and electronics repair on a car this complex. This is a pattern shared across the German luxury-sedan segment rather than specific to Mercedes, and it is the single biggest driver of the E-Class's steeper percentage depreciation relative to mainstream sedans, even though the absolute rupee value retained remains large.",
    "Diesel E 220d variants have historically commanded a firmer resale floor than petrol E 200 variants among India's luxury-sedan buyers, since high-mileage chauffeur-driven usage patterns favour diesel's lower running cost, and that preference has persisted even as diesel registration restrictions tighten in some metros."
  ],
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the resale value of a Mercedes-Benz E-Class in India?",
      "a": "A well-maintained E-Class retains roughly 65% of its ex-showroom price after two years, 55% after three years, and 41% after five years, as of September 2026. For a 2023 E 220d with 25,000-50,000 km, the current fair-market band is approximately ₹53.6-61.7 lakh."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does the diesel E 220d hold value better than the petrol E 200?",
      "a": "Yes. Across every year and km band in current data, the E 220d diesel trades roughly 10-11% above the equivalent E 200 petrol, reflecting stronger demand among high-mileage, chauffeur-driven buyers who prioritise diesel's lower running cost."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does the E-Class depreciate faster in percentage terms than mass-market sedans?",
      "a": "Luxury sedans like the E-Class lose value faster once the factory warranty expires, because used buyers price in higher expected service and parts costs. The E-Class retains about 41% at five years versus a segment benchmark of roughly 38%, so it actually holds a touch better than its luxury-sedan peers despite the steep overall curve."
    },
    {
      "q": "How much does an E-Class depreciate per year?",
      "a": "The E-Class loses roughly 22% of its value in year one and another 13-14% in year two, before settling into a steadier 8-9% annual decline from year three onward. Ten-year retention sits at around 20%."
    },
    {
      "q": "Which city gives the best resale price for an E-Class?",
      "a": "Delhi NCR and Mumbai currently trade at approximately +4% above the national mid, reflecting the deepest luxury-sedan buyer pools in the country. Kolkata trades at around -3%, where the used luxury-sedan market is comparatively thin."
    }
  ],
